name: needle in the binary
after: 21-bytes
intro: Binaries are not text, but they are full of text. Reading it is how you
intro: interrogate a program you cannot read the source of -- including the ones
intro: this very machine trusts.
---
q: count the printable strings in /bin/tlstunnel
a: strings /bin/tlstunnel | wc -l
ref: strings
cmp: ref
hint: strings pulls out the runs; wc -l just counts how many
---
setup: tlstunnel compiles its trust anchor INTO the binary rather than reading a
setup: system CA store, so the certificate authority's name is somewhere inside.
setup: That single baked-in anchor is the whole reason this machine trusts one
setup: issuer and no other.
q: show every string in /bin/tlstunnel mentioning Internet Security Research Group
a: strings /bin/tlstunnel | grep 'Internet Security Research Group'
ref: strings
cmp: ref
hint: pipe strings' output into grep for the phrase
---
setup: A digest is how you say "this exact binary, no other" in one line.
q: print the sha256 digest of /bin/busybox, and nothing else
a: sha256sum /bin/busybox | awk '{print $1}'
a: sha256sum /bin/busybox | cut -d' ' -f1
out: ^[0-9a-f]{64}$
ref: sha256sum
hint: sha256sum prints the digest then the filename; you only want field 1
